{#
  CASE STUDY SECTION STRIP — the second TOC.

  A case study is mostly pictures, so the notes' "on this page" panel cannot work here:
  it is a persistent column, and on a page of full-bleed imagery it sits ON the work.
  This is the same job solved the other way round — a bar pinned to the top of the
  window that appears once the reader is into the body, showing where they are and
  and offering a way to move that does not occupy the page while unused:

    title   a button; opens the full list ON DEMAND, so the list exists only while it
            is being looked at and can never cover an image

  ONE control, not three. There were prev/next steppers and an arrow-key shortcut too;
  both went. The list is the thing that actually answers "take me to a section", and
  three ways to do one job made a bar that reads as a toolbar rather than a label.

  Plus an IMAGE MODE switch, which belongs here rather than anywhere else: it is a way
  of reading this page, and this bar is where the page's reading controls live.

    headings — [{ id, text }] from craft.frontend.headingAnchors (h2s only)

  REAL ANCHORS underneath. The list is <a href="#id">, so without JavaScript the strip
  is a plain list of working links; case-toc.js only adds the pinning, the current
  marker and the mode switch.

  Ships `hidden`. It is a scroll affordance, and one sitting there before anyone has
  scrolled is just a bar across the top of a title page.
#}
{% if headings is defined and headings|length >= 2 %}
    <div class="c-case-toc" data-case-toc hidden>
        <div class="c-case-toc__bar">
            {# TWO insets, because that is how many the logo sits behind: `body > .b-wrap`
               pads the slab off the viewport, and the header's own .b-wrap pads the logo
               off the slab. This strip is FIXED, so it is outside both and has to
               reproduce them — the outer as .c-case-toc__page, the inner as the site's
               real .b-wrap class, whose padding steps up at md and lg. Hardcoding one
               number would line up at exactly one breakpoint. #}
            <div class="c-case-toc__page">
            <div class="b-wrap">
            <div class="c-case-toc__inner">
                {# The counter is what keeps the bar legible when the title truncates —
                   on a phone a long h2 loses its end, and "2/3" is the part that still
                   tells you where you are. #}
                <span class="c-case-toc__count" data-case-toc-count>1/{{ headings|length }}</span>

                <button
                    type="button"
                    class="c-case-toc__title"
                    data-case-toc-toggle
                    aria-expanded="false"
                    aria-controls="case-toc-list"
                >
                    <span class="c-case-toc__label" data-case-toc-label>{{ headings|first.text }}</span>
                    {# NO CARET ELEMENT. It is drawn on this button's own ::after (see
                       _case-toc.scss) — decoration doesn't earn markup, and an empty span
                       here would be an element that exists only to be coloured in. #}
                </button>

                {# aria-pressed, not a checkbox: this is a toggle on the view, and a
                   pressed button is how that is announced. #}
                <button
                    type="button"
                    class="c-case-toc__mode"
                    data-case-toc-mode
                    aria-pressed="false"
                    {# aria-label as well as the visible words, because that label is
                       hidden on a narrow screen where the section title needs the room —
                       without it the button would be announced as its icon, which is
                       aria-hidden, i.e. as nothing at all.

                       case-toc.js keeps the two IN STEP as the state changes. They have
                       to say the same thing: an accessible name that differs from the
                       visible text is the one thing a voice-control user cannot work
                       with, since they say what they can see. #}
                    aria-label="Image Mode: Off"
                >
                    <span class="c-case-toc__mode-label" data-case-toc-mode-label>Image Mode: Off</span>
                </button>
            </div>
            </div>
            </div>

            {# Reading progress through the article. It costs one element and earns its
               place twice over: it is the orientation that survives when the title is
               cut short. #}
            <div class="c-case-toc__progress" aria-hidden="true"><i data-case-toc-progress></i></div>
        </div>

        {# The list, closed. Only in the DOM as a panel — no fixed column, nothing over
           the work — and it closes on pick, on Escape and on a click outside. #}
        {# Same two insets as the bar above, so the list's numbers line up under the
           counter rather than hanging off some other edge. The sheet's white background
           stays full-bleed on the <ol>; only its contents are inset. #}
        <ol class="c-case-toc__list" id="case-toc-list" data-case-toc-list hidden>
            <div class="c-case-toc__page">
            <div class="b-wrap">
            {% for h in headings %}
                <li class="c-case-toc__item">
                    <a class="c-case-toc__link" href="#{{ h.id }}" data-case-toc-link>
                        <span class="c-case-toc__n">{{ loop.index }}.</span>{{ h.text }}
                    </a>
                </li>
            {% endfor %}
            </div>
            </div>
        </ol>
    </div>
{% endif %}
